\@doendnote{endnote1}{Notice that from now one we introduce the notation $[\protect \tilde  {m}]$ as the equivalence class of $\protect \tilde  {m}$ from $\protect \frac  {\protect \mathbb  {Z}}{n\protect \mathbb  {Z}}$.}
\@doendnote{endnote2}{Since $r$ is even $\protect \tilde  {m}^{r/2}$ is just a power of $m$.}
\@doendnote{endnote3}{Although $\protect \tilde  {m}^{r/2}+1$ is not necessirelly a divisor of $n$ it is highly probable this to be the case, in fact the probability of this appening increases with the size of $n$}
